<chapter xml:id="chapter-multiple-integrals-line-integrals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Multiple Integrals and Line Integrals</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> 
      This chapter develops integration beyond the single-variable setting.
      We begin with <em>multiple integrals</em> — double and triple integrals
      over planar and spatial domains — which provide the mathematical foundation
      for computing areas, volumes, masses, and moments of inertia of
      two- and three-dimensional bodies.
      We then turn to <em>line integrals</em>: integrals of scalar or vector
      fields along curves in the plane or in space.
      Line integrals arise naturally when computing arc lengths, work done by
      a force field along a path, or circulation and flux in fluid mechanics.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="double-triple-integrals/section.ptx"/>
  <xi:include href="curves-scalar-line-integrals/section.ptx"/>
